        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Négritude</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where students from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>’s colonies and territories met at the Clamart Salon of the Nardal sisters and founded L’Étudiant noir; Martinique, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and French Guiana, the homes of Césaire, Senghor and Damas; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>, seat of the Dakar School of art; the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Forerunners from Anténor Firmin (1885) to the Harlem Renaissance; the Clamart Salon and La Revue du Monde Noir (1931–32); the word négritude in L’Étudiant noir (1935); the anthologies of Damas (1946) and Senghor (1948), with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Sartre</a>’s “Black Orpheus”; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> (1960–1974); criticism from the 1960s and a scholarly renaissance in recent years.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a>, Martinican poet, playwright and politician, who coined the word; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a>, poet and first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>; Léon Damas, French Guianese poet; Abdoulaye Sadji; Paulette and Jane Nardal, whose salon brought the founders together; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a>, its interpreter; the painters Papa Ibra Tall, Ben Enwonwu and Wifredo Lam.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A literature and art of Black consciousness and pride that inspired Afro-Surrealism, Créolité, Antillanité and black is beautiful; its founders sought equality within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rather than independence; criticised from the 1960s as insufficiently militant or defensive.</span></div>
        </div>
