    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Négritude</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture.jpg" alt="Toussaint Louverture" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Toussaint Louverture <span class="kid-tale-cr">Pierre-Charles Baquoy (1759-1829), Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Black thinkers took pride in Haiti, where enslaved people rose up under Toussaint Louverture.</p><p>More than 90 years ago, Black students from France's colonies met in Paris, at a salon run by the Nardal sisters.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire.jpg" alt="Aimé Césaire" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Aimé Césaire <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jean Baptiste Devaux, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>There Aimé Césaire, from Martinique, turned nègre, an insulting French word, into a name of pride: Négritude.</p><p>He and his friends Senghor and Damas spoke out against colonial rule and racism.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor.jpg" alt="Léopold Sédar Senghor" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Léopold Sédar Senghor <span class="kid-tale-cr">Dominique Roger, CC BY-SA 3.0 igo</span></figcaption></figure><p>They said Black people had a history and culture as worthy as anyone else's.</p><p>Léopold Sédar Senghor, a poet, later became the first President of Senegal.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>In Senegal, painters of the Dakar School turned its ideas into art.</p><p>Some writers, such as Wole Soyinka, criticised it, saying that a tiger does not boast of being a tiger.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>It inspired movements in the Caribbean, and the idea &quot;black is beautiful&quot; in the United States.</p><p>Historians now say the Nardal sisters belong beside its founders, after their part was long overlooked.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Négritude took nègre, a derogatory French word, and made it a name of empowerment; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a> first used the word in its present sense in L’Étudiant noir in 1935.</li><li>Its founders, Césaire, Senghor and Damas, rejected colonialism, racism and assimilation, drew on Marxism and Surrealism, and celebrated African history, traditions and beliefs.</li><li>Paulette and Jane Nardal, who introduced the founders to the Harlem Renaissance in their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> salon, were long minimised in its history by the masculinist domination of the movement.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wole-soyinka">Wole Soyinka</a> mocked Négritude in a 1960 essay: “the duiker will not paint ‘duiker’ on his beautiful back to proclaim his duikeritude; you’ll know him by his elegant leap.”</p></aside>
    </section>

